# ===================================================================
# BLUEWATER — environment
# ===================================================================
# Copy to .env for local work (.env is gitignored), and set the same
# values in the Vercel project: Settings -> Environment Variables.
#
# Only DATABASE_URL is required. Without it the API answers 503 and the
# deck carries on exactly as it did before there was a database.
# ===================================================================

# ---- required ------------------------------------------------------
# Any Postgres: Neon, Vercel Postgres, Supabase, Railway, RDS, or one
# running locally. Use the provider's POOLED string here — a serverless
# function opens a connection per instance and a pooler is what keeps
# that from exhausting the connection limit.
DATABASE_URL="postgres://user:password@host/dbname?sslmode=require"

# ---- optional ------------------------------------------------------
# The direct, unpooled string. Only `npm run migrate` uses it: a pooler
# in transaction mode can refuse the multi-statement schema script.
# Leave unset and the migration falls back to DATABASE_URL.
# DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED="postgres://user:password@host/dbname?sslmode=require"

# Guards GET /api/viewers, which is the one endpoint that returns names,
# emails and phone numbers. Unset means that endpoint stays closed.
# Generate one: node -e "console.log(crypto.randomUUID())"
# ADMIN_TOKEN=""

# Set this and reads record a salted sha256 of the viewer's IP — useful
# for noticing a confidential deck being opened from somewhere it should
# not be. Unset means the column stays null, which is the default on
# purpose. Changing the salt later orphans every hash already stored.
#
# IF YOU TURN THIS ON, UPDATE WHAT THE READER IS TOLD. The sign-in card
# lists what is recorded and does not currently mention anything derived
# from an IP address, because by default nothing is. Setting this without
# amending that copy makes the deck's own disclosure incomplete. Grep the
# deck for "gt-small" to find it.
# IP_SALT=""

# Extra browser origins allowed to POST, comma separated. Any
# *.vercel.app, localhost, and storylandstudios.com are already allowed,
# so this is only needed if the deck gets proxied somewhere new.
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS="https://investors.example.com"

# Last resort, for a Postgres host with a private CA: encrypt the
# connection but stop verifying who is on the other end. Neon, Supabase
# and RDS do not need this.
# PGSSL_NO_VERIFY="0"
